From 380b7eaf6ded071c0a1e8cbabc3788bf64e615ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 20:17:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Encrypted DNS: a DNS-over-HTTPS server for devices, found
 through DHCP option 162 (102.8 line)

- rednsd answers DNS-over-HTTPS on TCP 443 of the LAN-side addresses beside DNS-over-TLS on 853,
  each with its own switch (reaper_doh_srv, reaper_dot_srv). One HTTP/2 parser: nghttp2, vendored
  under rednsd/nghttp2 (MIT, README.reaper), compiled into rednsd only. ALPN h2 alone; every limit
  a client can lean on is set here (streams, header list, window, flood and glitch rates, 64 KB of
  library memory a connection).
- Devices find it by themselves: dnsmasq publishes the router's encrypted DNS by DHCP option 162
  (RFC 9463) on every network whose DNS server is the router, only while the certificate's name
  and chain hold; the watchdog checks that every five minutes. DNS-over-HTTPS steps aside while
  Web Access from WAN over IPv6 uses port 443.
- The Encrypted DNS block is two switches, port 853 (DoT, DoQ) and DoH (443 to the known
  resolvers, their names); a router with the old single switch keeps both. They are read once per
  firewall build, and saving the block no longer restarts the device-facing server.
- rednsd: a connection whose device stopped reading is closed; header fields the library drops
  count against the header limit; a query with a second OPT record or a signature record is
  refused (it could carry another device's tag); a device DNS Director moves elsewhere loses its
  open connection; lookups inside one TLS record are all answered; a TCP retry matches its query
  by sequence number; a slot reused in one pass is skipped. When every slot is busy the device
  holding the most gives one up; an IPv6 /64 holds at most half; at most 16 new connections a
  pass, one neighbour lookup each. A chain that did not verify is checked again after a minute.
- rdnsmapd names the devices that use the router's DNS-over-TLS, for the Flow Explorer.
- The Encrypted DNS page shows both legs of a lookup, device to router and router to upstream,
  with a red warning on the leg that fails; the devices still on plain DNS; the DNS-over-HTTPS
  setup with its live address.
Also in these files: sdn.c answers a DHCP client without an address by broadcast (the dnsmasq
commit); encdns.sh no longer writes an error into its state file when no list is cached; the
Dreadwatch page reads the block's two switches.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
